{#
  The bridge's Python modules, ONE ConfigMap per monitor_bridge_modules entry, each holding the
  module under its flat key. The Deployment's projected `monitor-bridge-script` volume mounts
  every one back at its path under /app.

  Rendered here rather than staged on the node and applied by a task (D5's imperative-ConfigMap
  row, #5536), so the ConfigMaps are part of this role's manifests. Under Ansible a module edit
  changes the render and the shared role's rollout restart rolls the pod. Under Flux the
  renderer's `checksum/restart` hashes every ConfigMap the pod's projected volume names.
  scripts/renderer/tests/test_renderer_monitor_bridge_script.py holds both halves.

  One per module, not one for all: k8s/manifests applies client-side, which copies each object
  into a `last-applied-configuration` annotation capped at 262144 bytes. The modules total about
  700 KB as JSON and the largest package about 200 KB, so neither one ConfigMap nor one per
  package stays clear of the cap as the bridge grows. One module stays far under it, because
  modules split at 600 lines. The renderer test holds every one under half the cap.

  The name derivation (`.py` dropped, `/` and `_` to `-`) is repeated in deployment.yaml.j2;
  the renderer test fails when a projected source names a ConfigMap this file does not render.

  `lookup('file')` strips trailing whitespace, and the `|` block puts back exactly one newline,
  so each key equals its source file's bytes. The lookup's text is not templated again, so the
  PromQL braces in the modules reach the pod as written.

  Keep comments at column 0 and above the documents: an indented comment between two keys would
  land inside the preceding block scalar.
#}
{% for module in monitor_bridge_modules %}
---
apiVersion: v1
kind: ConfigMap
metadata:
  name: monitor-bridge-script-{{ module[:-3] | replace('/', '-') | replace('_', '-') }}
  namespace: {{ k8s_namespace }}
data:
  {{ module | replace('/', '_') }}: |
{{ lookup('file', playbook_dir ~ '/roles/k8s/monitor-bridge/files/' ~ module) | indent(4, true) }}
{% endfor %}
